From: Thorch contributors
Subject: [PATCH] input: qcom-hv-haptics: honour FF stop requests in playback

haptics_playback() returns 0 for a stop request without touching the
hardware, so a stop that arrives through the FF interface never reaches
the motor:

	if (!!val && !chip->chip_is_playing) {
		...
		return haptics_enable_play(chip, true);
	}

	return 0;

That breaks any client that reuses one uploaded effect. stop_work is
scheduled by haptics_upload_effect() at UPLOAD time for replay.length
milliseconds, not per play, so the first play after the timer has already
expired is never stopped: not by the ff-core replay timer, because that
also arrives through haptics_playback() as val = 0, and not by
EVIOCSPLAY(0), for the same reason. Only the calibration, sweep and
stop_work paths, which call haptics_enable_play(chip, false) directly,
ever stopped the motor.

The AYN Thor's on-screen keyboard hits this on every tap. gamepad-osk
uploads a single FF_CONSTANT effect when it opens the device and then
plays that same id for every keypress, so the 300 ms stop_work armed at
upload expired long before the first tap. The first tap started the LRA
and nothing could stop it, which is what the user felt as a motor that
would not shut off.

Turn a stop request into the same direct call the working paths already
use, but only while playing, so a late or duplicate stop stays a no-op
and does not clear PLAY_EN or wait for a brake that was never started.

Fixes: 1002-haptics-driver-support-periodic-sine-and-fixes.patch
---
 drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -2858,6 +2858,9 @@
 		return haptics_enable_play(chip, true);
 	}

+	if (!val && chip->chip_is_playing)
+		return haptics_enable_play(chip, false);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
